<DocSection Title="Before you start">
    <p>This page assumes you've already:</p>
    <ul>
        <li>Installed the prerequisites and template — <MudLink Href="/get-started/installation">Installation</MudLink>.</li>
        <li>Scaffolded a project with <code>dotnet new shift</code> — <MudLink Href="/get-started/template-parameters">Template parameters</MudLink>.</li>
        <li>Have a SQL Server instance running locally. <b>LocalDB</b> (ships with Visual Studio / SQL Server Express LocalDB) is the easiest option for development.</li>
    </ul>
</DocSection>

<DocSection Title="Step 1: Configure the connection string">
    <p>
        Open <code>YourApp.API/appsettings.Development.json</code>. The template ships
        with a connection string pointing at <code>localhost\sqlexpress</code> — that
        won't exist on most machines. Change <code>SQLServer</code> to point at
        whatever SQL Server you actually have running.
    </p>

    <p>For LocalDB, the connection string looks like this:</p>
    <DocSnippet Name="LocalDbConnectionString" Language="json" />

    <h3>Cosmos DB: switch it off unless the emulator is running</h3>
    <p>
        The same file also has <code>"CosmosDb": { "Enabled": true }</code>. With it on,
        the app copies some records to Cosmos DB, which in development means the
        <b>Azure Cosmos DB Emulator</b> on <code>localhost:8081</code>. If the emulator isn't
        running, the app still works — but the console shows red <code>fail:</code> lines
        such as <code>CosmosDB Syncing Failed … (localhost:8081)</code>. Unless you're trying
        out Cosmos replication, switch it off in the same file:
    </p>
    <DocSnippet Name="DisableCosmos" Language="json" />
</DocSection>

<DocSection Title="Step 2: Create the initial migration">
    <p>
        The template ships with an empty <code>Migrations</code> folder by design.
        Your first job is to create an initial migration that captures the entire
        schema (your entities plus all the framework's identity tables) so EF Core
        has something to apply.
    </p>

    <p>From your project's root directory:</p>
    <DocSnippet Name="CreateInitialMigration" Language="bash" />

    <p>
        The project has a single <code>DbContext</code>, <code>DB</code>, so
        <code>--context DB</code> is optional; it is spelled out here only to be explicit.
    </p>

    <DocCallout Type="DocCalloutType.Tip">
        If you ever modify your entities later (add a property, add an entity, change
        a relationship), repeat this step with a different migration name —
        <code>dotnet ef migrations add AddProductCategoryColor --context DB ...</code> — and
        then re-run Step 3 below.
    </DocCallout>
</DocSection>

